# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — it no longer exists in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone** — no equivalent needed since abort can't happen
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch blocks for it are dead code

## The Core Problem

Your `Stop()` relies on `Thread.Abort()` to interrupt a blocking `GetNextItem()` call. In .NET 8, you must use **cooperative cancellation** via `CancellationToken`.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — roll back partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        timeout ??= TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);

        // Signal cancellation
        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait with timeout — replaces the Thread.Sleep + Abort pattern
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(timeout.Value)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log warning; task will complete
            // when the next cancellation-aware call observes the token
            Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## If You Can't Make `GetNextItem` Async

When wrapping legacy synchronous blocking code that doesn't accept a `CancellationToken`:

```csharp
private object GetNextItemCancellable(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Option 1: Poll with short timeouts instead of blocking indefinitely
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        if (TryGetNextItem(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(500), out var item))
            return item;
    }
    throw new OperationCanceledException(token);

    // Option 2 (last resort): Let the thread "leak" on timeout
    // — never use Thread.Interrupt(), it has similar issues to Abort
}
```

## Common Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---------|-------------|
| **Catching `ThreadAbortException`** | Dead code in .NET 8 — remove or replace with `OperationCanceledException` |
| **Using `Thread.Sleep` for shutdown coordination** | Use `Task.WhenAny` with a timeout or `Task.Delay` + cancellation |
| **Forgetting to pass `CancellationToken` into blocking calls** | The whole pattern breaks if the blocking call can't be interrupted |
| **Using `Thread.Interrupt()`** as a substitute | Throws `ThreadInterruptedException` only during `Wait`/`Sleep` — unreliable and still not cooperative |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Always store the `Task` so you can await it during shutdown |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Implement `IDisposable` on the host class |

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread` + `volatile bool` → `Task` + `CancellationTokenSource`
2. Replace `Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`
3. Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
4. Remove `Thread.ResetAbort()` — no equivalent needed
5. Make blocking calls accept `CancellationToken` (the hardest part)
6. Add `IDisposable` to clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`
